  ],
  "details": "<p>The name of this city district in western Babylon is corrupt, damaged, or illegible in all extant and identified copies of <a href=\"http://oracc.museum.upenn.edu/btto/Q004801\"><span>Tintir = Babylon V</span></a> (line 99). Because it did not have any temples, shrines, or daises, it is not mentioned in <a href=\"http://oracc.museum.upenn.edu/btto/Q000305\"><span>Tintir = Babylon IV</span></a>. nu-x-\u00f9/nu-HAR-UD is the westernmost city district and it appears to have been bounded on the south, west, and north by the walls <a href=\"../202833933\"><span>Imgur-Enlil</span></a> and <a href=\"../897225060\"><span>N\u0113metti-Enlil</span></a> (with access in and out of the inner city by the <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../223954669\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Adad Gate</a> and probably also the <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../897449051\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">King\u2019s Gate</a>) and on the west by the <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../633091640\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">B\u0101b-Lugalerra</a>, <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../633091639\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Kumar</a>, and <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"../89975947\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Tuba</a> districts. According to <span>Tintir = Babylon V</span> (line 99), the western and eastern limits of nu-x-\u00f9/nu-HAR-UD were the Adad Gate and Akus Gate (which formed part of the former, Old Babylonian city wall).</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "According to the Babylonian topographical text Tintir = Babylon, nu-x-\u00f9/nu-HAR-UD (name fragmentary and not reconstructable) was one of the four city districts of the western half of the inner city of Babylon.",
